{"cuisine/culture-of-guyana-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/culture-of-guyana-cuisine","n":"Guyanese cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Guyana","intro":[["Guyanese culture reflects the influence of Indian, African, Amerindian, British, Portuguese, Chinese, Creole, and Dutch cultures. Guyana is part of the mainland Caribbean region. Guyanese culture shares a continuum with the cultures of islands in the West Indies."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"culture-of-guyana-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Guyana#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Metemgee","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"metemgee.com","note":"Guyanese-born Althea Brown's blog of Guyanese and Caribbean recipes.","url":"https://metemgee.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Alica's Pepperpot","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"alicaspepperpot.com","note":"Blog of Guyanese home recipes.","url":"https://alicaspepperpot.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Tastes Like Home: My Caribbean Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Cynthia Nelson","note":"Guyanese and Caribbean home cooking by a Guyanese food writer, published by Ian Randle Publishers.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/cambodian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cambodian-cuisine","n":"Cambodian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Cambodia","intro":[["Cambodian cuisine is the national cuisine of Cambodia. It reflects the varied culinary traditions of different ethnic groups in Cambodia, central of which is Khmer cuisine, the nearly-two-thousand-year-old culinary tradition of the Khmer people. Over centuries, Cambodian cuisine has incorporated elements of Indian, Chinese (in particular Teochew), French, and Portuguese cuisines. Due to some of these shared influences and mutual interaction, Cambodian cuisine has many similarities with the cuisines of Central Thailand, and Southern Vietnam and to a lesser extent also Central Vietnam, Northeastern Thailand and Laos."],["Cambodian cuisine can be categorized into three main types: rural, elite and royal cuisine. Although there is some distinction between royal and popular cuisine, it is not as pronounced as in Thailand and Laos. Cambodian royal dishes tend to feature a wider variety of higher-quality ingredients and contain more meat."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cambodian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambodian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Cha kroeung","dish/cha-kroeung"],["Chha kh'nhei","dish/chha-kh-nhei"],["Grilled honeycomb","dish/grilled-honeycomb"],["Kampot pepper crab","dish/kampot-pepper-crab"],["Thali","dish/thali"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Chef Nak","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chefnak.com","note":"Site of Cambodian chef and cookbook author Chef Nak, with notes on Khmer cookbooks.","url":"https://www.chefnak.com/nhum","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"My Cambodia: A Khmer Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nite Yun","note":"Khmer recipes from the chef behind Nyum Bai in Oakland.","url":"https://www.penguin.co.nz/books/my-cambodia-9781984863379","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781984863379"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Spring onions","ingredient/spring-onions"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Ginger root","ingredient/ginger-root"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Honeycomb","ingredient/honeycomb"],["Crab","ingredient/crab"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Kampot pepper","ingredient/kampot-pepper"],["Groat","ingredient/groat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"More from Cambodia","count":1,"links":[["Cambodian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/cambodian-chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a9/Neary_Khmer%2C_2018-01-02_%28002%29.jpg/500px-Neary_Khmer%2C_2018-01-02_%28002%29.jpg","w":500,"h":320,"by":"Saphabodi","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neary_Khmer%2C_2018-01-02_(002).jpg"}},"cuisine/baden-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/baden-cuisine","n":"Baden cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Germany","intro":[["The cuisine of the German region of Baden is comparatively light and small among Germany's regional cuisines, but is regarded within the country as among the best. Baden has the highest density of starred-restaurants in Germany."],["Baden is situated in volcanic soils and has Germany's warmest climate, permitting strong agricultural production of tobacco, wine, fruit and horticulture, including asparagus and chestnuts. Close proximity to France and Switzerland exert strong influences, including on home-cooking dishes. Some of dishes typical of the region include Baeckeoffe and Flammkuchen, Sauerkraut or Schäufele. Spätzle and egg pasta are made, using more eggs than in the neighboring region of Swabia. The mix of hearty and sweet dishes was adapted from Palatine cuisine, as was the voluminous use of a range of wines in a meal."],["The region's fruits support a variety of fruity desserts and pastries, eaten at the German \"Kaffee und Kuchen\" (lit. \"coffee and cake\", similar to the British tea time). According to legend, the Baden city of Tübingen was where Black Forest gateau originated."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"baden-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baden_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Farina","ingredient/farina"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/71/Badische_Maultaschen.jpg/500px-Badische_Maultaschen.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Brücke-Osteuropa","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badische_Maultaschen.jpg"}},"ingredient/thymus":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/thymus","n":"Thymus","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The genus Thymus contains about 350 species of aromatic perennial herbaceous plants and subshrubs in the family Lamiaceae. It is native to the Mediterranean area of Europe, North Africa and Turkey."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-thymus"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thymus_(plant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Babbouche","dish/babbouche"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Elettaria cardamomum","ingredient/elettaria-cardamomum"],["Liquorice","ingredient/liquorice"],["Mentha pulegium","ingredient/mentha-pulegium"],["Ras el hanout","ingredient/ras-el-hanout"],["Snail","ingredient/snail"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/1b/Thymus_camphoratus_kz07.jpg/500px-Thymus_camphoratus_kz07.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thymus_camphoratus_kz07.jpg"}},"dish/baumkuchen":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/baumkuchen","n":"Baumkuchen","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Baumkuchen is a kind of spit cake from German cuisine. It is also a popular dessert in Japan. The characteristic rings that appear in its slices resemble tree rings, and give the cake its German name, Baumkuchen, which literally translates to \"tree cake\" or \"log cake\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-baumkuchen"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baumkuchen"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Quick German Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"quick-german-recipes.com","note":"Long-running site by German-born cook Gerhild Fulson (Oma) with traditional German home recipes.","url":"https://www.quick-german-recipes.com/about-me.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Dirndl Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dirndlkitchen.com","note":"Blog of German and Bavarian recipes, from main dishes to holiday baking.","url":"https://dirndlkitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Quick German Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"quick-german-recipes.com","note":"Thuringia page on German-born cook Gerhild Fulson's long-running German recipe site.","url":"https://www.quick-german-recipes.com/thuringia.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from German cuisine","links":[["Bockwurst","dish/bockwurst"],["Buchteln","dish/buchteln"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chrain","dish/chrain"],["Dampfnudel","dish/dampfnudel"],["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"],["Egg puffs and so