from pernil, a Latin cuisine roast pork, its creators and namesake are the chola street vendors of La Paz (chola paceña)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chola-sandwich"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chola_sandwich"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Bolivian cuisine","cuisine/bolivian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Escabeche","ingredient/escabeche"],["Pernil","ingredient/pernil"],["Torresmo","ingredient/torresmo"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Chipa by the Dozen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chipabythedozen.com","note":"Bolivian-American Lizet Bowen's bilingual blog of Bolivian recipes.","url":"https://chipabythedozen.com/en/category/bolivian-food-recipes-savory-sweet/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Bolivian cuisine","links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Coca tea","dish/coca-tea"],["Locro","dish/locro"],["Salteña","dish/saltena"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/52/Sandwich_de_chola.jpg/500px-Sandwich_de_chola.jpg","w":500,"h":453,"by":"Caleidoscopic","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandwich_de_chola.jpg"}},"ingredient/cinnamomum-verum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cinnamomum-verum","n":"Cinnamomum verum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Cinnamomum verum (synonym Cinnamomum zeylanicum), also known as true cinnamon or Ceylon cinnamon, is a small evergreen tree belonging to the family Lauraceae, native to Sri Lanka. The inner bark of the tree is historically regarded as the spice cinnamon, though this name has also been used for C. cassia (cassia) as well."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cinnamomum-verum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinnamomum_verum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Roti gambang","dish/roti-gambang"]]],["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Tarta de Santiago","dish/tarta-de-santiago"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Lime","ingredient/lime"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/e/e1/Cinnamomum_verum1.jpg","w":500,"h":323,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cinnamomum_verum1.jpg"}},"ingredient/liver":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/liver","n":"Liver","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The liver of mammals, fowl, and fish is commonly eaten as food by humans (see offal). Pork, lamb, veal, beef, chicken, goose, and cod livers are widely available from butchers and supermarkets while stingray and burbot livers are common in some European countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-liver"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liver_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Domestic duck","ingredient/domestic-duck"],["Domestic goose","ingredient/domestic-goose"],["Liver as food","ingredient/liver-as-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/2f/Pig%27s_liver_with_sauteed_onion.jpg/500px-Pig%27s_liver_with_sauteed_onion.jpg","w":500,"h":403,"by":"FotoosVanRobin from Netherlands","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pig's_liver_with_sauteed_onion.jpg"}},"ingredient/big-intestine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/big-intestine","n":"Big intestine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The large intestine, also known as the large bowel, is the last part of the gastrointestinal tract and of the digestive system in tetrapods. Water and salts are absorbed here and the remaining waste material is stored in the rectum as feces before being removed by defecation. Although it is shorter than the small intestine, it is called the large intestine because it is wider in diameter."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-big-intestine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_intestine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Small intestine","ingredient/small-intestine"]]}]},"dish/iris-pastry":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/iris-pastry","n":"Iris (pastry)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sicilian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Iris is a Sicilian pastry consisting of a brioscia that is filled with a sweet and creamy filling containing ricotta cheese, coated with breadcrumbs and deep fried."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-iris-pastry"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(pastry)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"]]},{"h":"More from Sicilian cuisine","links":[["Busiate","dish/busiate"],["Cassatella di Agira","dish/cassatella-di-agira"],["Crocchè","dish/crocche"],["Maccu","dish/maccu"],["Panella","dish/panella"],["Pani câ meusa","dish/pani-ca-meusa"],["Parmigiana","dish/parmigiana"],["Pasta alla Norma","dish/pasta-alla-norma"],["Pasta con le sarde","dish/pasta-con-le-sarde"],["Pasta mollicata","dish/pasta-mollicata"],["Stigghiola","dish/stigghiola"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/64/Iris_cioccolato_e_panna_2.jpg/500px-Iris_cioccolato_e_panna_2.jpg","w":500,"h":666,"by":"Auregann","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iris_cioccolato_e_panna_2.jpg"}},"dish/krechek":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/krechek","n":"Krechek","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Krechek or krecek or sambal goreng krechek is a traditional Javanese cattle skin spicy stew dish from Yogyakarta and Central Java, Indonesia. Traditionally it is made from the soft inner skin of cattle (cow or water buffalo), however, the most common recipe today uses readily available rambak or krupuk kulit (cattle skin crackers)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-krechek"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krechek"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Cowhide","ingredient/cowhide"],["Krupuk kulit","ingredient/krupuk-kulit"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","li